{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018yevdovb2xoFcUDngRZdsR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: \"Claim 1 collapses as headline — architecture stratification reveals 31% temporal DIF was population heterogeneity artifact (decoder-only C% drops to 5-14% with RMSEA>0.05). Paper reframes as negative-result contribution: 'naive psychometric DIF transfer to LLM populations fails due to architecture heterogeneity and multidimensional construct violation; we empirically characterize why.' Surviving pillars: (1) the negative result itself documenting structural failure modes, (2) Claim 2 ground truth meta-critique (method-independent), (3) Claim 3 semi-synthetic framework as reusable infrastructure. Target ICLR 2027 as empirical analysis paper with intellectual honesty.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n    plan_id: plan_001\n    description: \"Full-population DIF on METABENCH MMLU — this experiment still yields ~31% C% because it uses the architecture-mixed population. The result is not 'wrong' but is later revealed by plan_010 to be an artifact of architecture heterogeneity.\"\n    expected_results:\n      temporal_ets_c_pct: 32.1\n      temporal_fpr_li_clean: 0.325\n      temporal_enrichment: 0.97\n      ability_ets_c_pct: 3.1\n      ability_enrichment: 1.15\n      within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 58.7\n      within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct: 41.2\n      domain_spearman_rho: 0.29\n      domain_spearman_p: 0.028\n      sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.21\n      temporal_ability_jaccard: 0.016\n      negative_disc_sensitivity_delta: 1.8\n    result_rationale: \"Full-population DIF operates on architecture-heterogeneous sample mixing encoder-decoder, decoder-only, and MoE models. Under worst-case, this heterogeneity is the PRIMARY driver of the 31% C% — different architectures respond to items differently not because of contamination or temporal drift, but because their information processing creates systematic DIF when pooled. The 31% is 'real' statistically but an artifact of population mixing. Enrichment≈1.0 because DIF flags are architecture-driven, orthogonal to web-overlap. Within-family DIF (Llama-2 vs Llama-3) remains high (~59%) because version jumps involve architecture changes (GQA, RoPE scaling) not just continued training. Sanity check remains near zero because random split preserves architecture proportions in both groups.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DIF detection by cohort definition × item filtering (4 conditions). Temporal cohort shows 30-33% C% regardless of negative-discrimination filtering — the signal is architecture-driven, not item-quality-driven.\"\n      - \"Table: Within-family DIF for qualifying pairs. Llama-2→3 shows 59% (major architecture revision), Mistral→Mixtral 41% (MoE transition). Both reflect architecture changes, not contamination.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: DIF effect size (Δ_MH) vs domain accuracy improvement. ρ=0.29 appears moderate but will be shown null after architecture control (plan_010).\"\n      - \"Heatmap: C% by MMLU subject × cohort definition. Temporal cohort shows uniformly high C% across all subjects — consistent with a global confound (architecture) rather than domain-specific contamination.\"\n    analysis: \"Results appear to support Claim 1 at face value — 31% temporal instability, non-trivial sanity check. However, the uniformity across domains (no strong clustering in 'known contaminated' subjects) and the within-family results tracking architecture changes rather than training data changes are early warning signs. The enrichment≈1.0 result is correctly interpreted as expected (proxy mismatch), but will later be reinterpreted: DIF flags don't correlate with ANYTHING meaningful because they're architecture noise. This experiment provides the 'naive application' baseline that plan_010 deconstructs.\"\n\n  - name: rl_robustness_validation\n    plan_id: plan_002\n    description: \"RL robustness validation becomes uninformative because the underlying DIF method is misspecified. Group-level DIF on architecture-mixed cohorts with or without RL models produces similarly inflated C% — testing 'robustness' of a broken method.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sft_only_cohort_c_pct: 29.4\n      alignment_rl_cohort_c_pct: 30.8\n      reasoning_rl_added_cohort_c_pct: 31.5\n      dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_rl: 0.72\n      auroc_dif_vs_constat: 0.53\n      auroc_dif_vs_mink: 0.51\n      auroc_ci_width: 0.18\n      delong_p_dif_vs_constat: 0.61\n      delong_p_dif_vs_mink: 0.74\n      tost_equivalence_p: 0.08\n      power_analysis_detection_boundary_p: 0.55\n      n_reasoning_rl_models_available: 13\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, DIF produces ~30% C% regardless of cohort composition because the signal is architecture-driven, not RL-driven. Adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a focal cohort of 656 models changes composition negligibly — C% moves by <2pp. The 'stability' across cohort compositions (sft/alignment-rl/reasoning-rl) is not evidence of robustness but of the method's insensitivity to the actual manipulation. AUROC comparisons against ConStat/Min-K% are near chance (0.51-0.53) because DIF flags are effectively random w.r.t. actual contamination. TOST equivalence test marginally fails (p=0.08) because the comparison is underpowered with N=13 RL models, not because there's a real difference. The entire experiment is measuring properties of a misspecified method — 'robust' misspecification is not a virtue.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: C% by cohort composition (SFT-only, +Alignment-RL, +Reasoning-RL). Shows <2pp variation — interpretable as robustness OR as insensitivity to meaningful signal.\"\n      - \"Table: Per-model detection comparison (DIF flag membership vs ConStat/Min-K% scores) for 13 reasoning-RL models. Near-chance discrimination.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curves: DIF vs ConStat vs Min-K% on the 13 reasoning-RL models. All curves near diagonal — none of the methods reliably detect contamination at the individual model level with N=13.\"\n      - \"Power curve: Detection probability vs exploitation rate p. Shows DIF 'detection boundary' at p≈0.55, but this is against injected contamination (plan_011 style), not real RL-concealed contamination.\"\n    analysis: \"The experiment is interpretable two ways: (1) DIF is robust to RL (flags same items regardless of RL presence), or (2) DIF flags are architecture noise unaffected by any real signal. Under worst-case, interpretation (2) dominates. The N=13 reasoning-RL sample is too small for definitive conclusions either way, which is acknowledged. The AUROC comparisons show DIF has no advantage over other methods for per-model detection — but this was never DIF's design (group-level detector). The entire premise of 'RL robustness' becomes moot when the method doesn't detect real contamination in the first place. This experiment converts from a strength argument to an additional data point in the negative-result narrative.\"\n\n  - name: constat_perturbation_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003a\n    description: \"ConStat perturbation testing provides moderate standalone detection on non-RL models, but DIF comparison is meaningless because DIF's flags are architecture artifacts.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_reformulated: 217\n      constat_auroc_non_rl: 0.68\n      constat_auroc_rl: 0.44\n      constat_fdr_controlled_detections: 34\n      constat_detection_rate: 0.157\n      dif_constat_overlap_pct: 18\n      dif_constat_jaccard: 0.09\n      reformulation_quality_human_rating: 4.1/5\n      effect_size_distribution_median: 0.42\n      effect_size_distribution_iqr: [0.18, 0.71]\n    result_rationale: \"ConStat operates on a fundamentally different mechanism (within-model perturbation sensitivity) than DIF (between-group differential). Under worst-case, ConStat shows moderate AUROC (0.68) on non-RL models because perturbation sensitivity is a real contamination signal — memorized answers are brittle to reformulation. The RL degradation (0.44) confirms Wang et al.'s theoretical prediction. The critical finding is the near-zero overlap between ConStat detections and DIF flags (Jaccard=0.09). Under the 'best' interpretation, this means they detect complementary signals. Under worst-case, it means DIF flags are random w.r.t. actual contamination (confirmed by enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al.). ConStat provides standalone value but the DIF-ConStat complementarity narrative collapses — you can't build an ensemble when one component is noise.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: ConStat detection rates by effect size bin (small/medium/large). Large effects detected at 65%, medium at 28%, small at 4%.\"\n      - \"Table: Cross-tabulation of DIF flags × ConStat detections. Near-independence (χ²=1.3, p=0.25) — consistent with DIF being architecture noise.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve: ConStat performance stratified by model type (base, SFT, alignment-RL, reasoning-RL). Clear degradation curve from base (0.74) to reasoning-RL (0.44).\"\n      - \"Venn diagram: DIF-C items vs ConStat detections. Minimal overlap, visually reinforcing independence.\"\n    analysis: \"ConStat provides a legitimate contamination signal (perturbation sensitivity is mechanistically grounded) but lives in a different detection paradigm than DIF. Under worst-case, this experiment inadvertently strengthens the case that DIF flags are not contamination-related — an independent contamination detector shows near-zero agreement with DIF. For the reframed paper, ConStat results join the 'what works' section while DIF joins the 'what fails and why' section. ConStat's RL vulnerability (AUROC 0.44) remains a real limitation that the paper can discuss.\"\n\n  - name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003b\n    description: \"DVD generation variance shows bimodal contamination signal for verbatim memorization but cannot be meaningfully compared to DIF because DIF is misspecified.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_models_evaluated: 12\n      n_items_evaluated: 500\n      dvd_auroc_verbatim: 0.61\n      dvd_auroc_variant: 0.47\n      bimodal_detection_rate: 0.083\n      generation_variance_clean_mean: 0.34\n      generation_variance_contaminated_mean: 0.21\n      dif_dvd_overlap_pct: 14\n      dif_dvd_jaccard: 0.06\n      temperature_sensitivity: \"T=0.7 AUROC=0.58, T=1.0 AUROC=0.61\"\n      compute_cost_per_model_hours: 6.2\n    result_rationale: \"DVD detects verbatim memorization (low generation variance = deterministic recall of memorized answer) at moderate AUROC (0.61). Variant contamination (paraphrased training exposure) is undetectable (0.47, below chance noise). The bimodal detection rate (8.3%) is low because most models don't exhibit extreme verbatim memorization on MMLU — MMLU items are widely available, so 'contamination' is ubiquitous and doesn't produce sharp variance signatures. DIF-DVD overlap (Jaccard=0.06) is near zero, again consistent with DIF flags being architecture noise rather than contamination signal. The compute cost (6.2 GPU-hours per model × 12 models ≈ 74 hours) is within budget but limits scalability. Under worst-case, DVD provides mild standalone signal but is insufficient for the ensemble premise.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DVD detection metrics by contamination type (verbatim vs variant vs clean). Only verbatim shows separation.\"\n      - \"Table: Generation variance distributions per model (12 models × summary stats). Models with known training data overlap show slightly lower variance but effect is small.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Histogram: Generation variance scores for known-contaminated vs clean items. Mild leftward shift for contaminated items, heavy overlap.\"\n      - \"Bimodal detection example: one model showing clear bimodal variance distribution on ~20 items (cherry-picked best case).\"\n    analysis: \"DVD's theoretical mechanism (memorized items produce low generation variance) is sound but MMLU's universal exposure means the contrast between 'contaminated' and 'clean' items is weak. Under worst-case, DVD provides marginal standalone signal (AUROC 0.61) — better than DIF's effective chance performance but insufficient to carry a detection claim. The near-zero DIF overlap is another nail in DIF's coffin. For the reframed paper, DVD becomes a 'methods we evaluated' entry rather than an ensemble component.\"\n\n  - name: ensemble_fusion\n    plan_id: plan_003c\n    description: \"Ensemble fusion fails because the DIF component is noise and the remaining two signals (ConStat + DVD) are individually weak, producing an ensemble barely above the better single method.\"\n    expected_results:\n      ensemble_auroc_2of3: 0.52\n      ensemble_auroc_weighted: 0.63\n      ensemble_auroc_constat_dvd_only: 0.66\n      ensemble_fpr_2of3: 0.04\n      ensemble_fpr_weighted: 0.11\n      dif_weight_optimized: 0.03\n      constat_weight_optimized: 0.62\n      dvd_weight_optimized: 0.35\n      cross_val_auroc_std: 0.09\n      uniform_weight_auroc: 0.55\n      ensemble_vs_constat_alone_improvement: 0.02\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, the ensemble is dominated by ConStat because DIF's optimized weight drops to ~0.03 (effectively excluded by cross-validation). 2-of-3 majority voting produces AUROC=0.52 because DIF is a random voter — it agrees with the correct answer by chance 50% of the time, degrading consensus. Weighted fusion (0.63) barely exceeds ConStat alone (0.68 standalone vs 0.66 in the ConStat+DVD pair) because DVD adds noise rather than complementary signal for non-verbatim contamination. The ensemble doesn't exceed the best single component — a classic sign that the 'complementary coverage' hypothesis is wrong. Cross-validation variance (std=0.09) is high because the small calibration set (few items with known contamination) produces unstable weights. This experiment's failure is a direct consequence of DIF's misspecification propagating through the ensemble.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Ensemble performance by configuration (2-of-3, weighted, ConStat+DVD only, each alone). Shows no ensemble benefit over best single method.\"\n      - \"Table: Cross-validated weights across 5 folds. DIF weight ranges 0.00-0.08 across folds — consistently suppressed.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curves: Individual methods vs ensemble configurations. Ensemble curves cluster around ConStat's curve, never exceeding it significantly.\"\n      - \"Calibration plot: Predicted contamination probability vs observed rate. Poor calibration due to small calibration set.\"\n    analysis: \"The ensemble premise required three complementary detectors covering different contamination regimes. Under worst-case, this collapses: DIF is architecture noise (not a contamination detector at all), DVD is marginal (AUROC 0.61), and only ConStat provides real signal (AUROC 0.68 on non-RL). The ensemble reduces to 'ConStat with extra steps.' For the reframed paper, this negative result strengthens the 'DIF doesn't work for this purpose' narrative — even optimistic fusion cannot rescue a misspecified component. The experiment is dropped from claims but reported in supplementary as additional evidence for the negative result.\"\n\n  - name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n    plan_id: plan_004\n    description: \"GSM8K replication still shows semi-synthetic framework works (infrastructure contribution survives) but temporal DIF on GSM8K is reinterpreted as reflecting GSM8K's simpler architecture landscape.\"\n    expected_results:\n      step1_gsm8k_temporal_c_pct: 12.3\n      step1_gsm8k_external_c_pct: 38.7\n      step1_gsm8k_sanity_c_pct: 0.4\n      step2_standard_delta_tpr_p10: 0.78\n      step2_external_delta_tpr_p10: 0.49\n      step3_n_models: 33\n      step3_pearson_r: -0.31\n      step3_pearson_p: 0.08\n      step3_spearman_rho: -0.28\n      step3_spearman_p: 0.11\n      step3_partial_r_controlling_accuracy: -0.22\n      step3_partial_p: 0.21\n      step3_permutation_p: 0.09\n      step3_ci_95_lower: -0.58\n      step3_ci_95_upper: 0.03\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, GSM8K temporal DIF drops to 12.3% (vs MMLU's 31%) because GSM8K's model population is more architecturally homogeneous (primarily decoder-only math models). This actually supports the architecture-artifact hypothesis — less architecture mixing → less spurious DIF. The semi-synthetic framework (Step 2) still shows ΔTPR=+0.78 because injection creates real differential signal detectable by MH regardless of architecture mixing — this confirms the framework works even if natural DIF is artifactual. Step 3 (GSM1K correlation) weakens substantially: r=-0.31 with p=0.08 (marginally non-significant), CI crossing zero [-0.58, 0.03]. The N=33 sample that was borderline in the 'best' scenario becomes non-significant under worst-case measurement noise. Partial correlation controlling accuracy drops to r=-0.22, p=0.21 — clearly non-significant. The GSM1K finding cannot carry any weight in the paper.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: GSM8K DIF summary statistics vs MMLU DIF. Lower C% on more architecturally homogeneous population supports architecture confound explanation.\"\n      - \"Table: GSM1K correlation analysis with confidence intervals. CI crosses zero — finding is suggestive at best.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: Per-model contamination gap vs DIF-C item performance. Trend line with wide CI band crossing zero. Individual points show high scatter.\"\n      - \"Dose-response curve: Semi-synthetic ΔTPR vs injection probability p. Clear monotonic increase — framework validation survives regardless of natural DIF interpretation.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment provides mixed evidence under worst-case. The semi-synthetic framework (Claim 3) is fully validated — injection creates detectable signal, confirming the infrastructure works. But temporal DIF on GSM8K (12.3%) aligns with the architecture-artifact story (less architecture mixing → less spurious DIF). GSM1K correlation becomes non-significant (p=0.08, CI crosses zero), removing the cross-benchmark contamination validation. For the reframed paper: framework portability (Step 2) is a positive result supporting Claim 3; temporal DIF comparison (Step 1) becomes additional evidence for the architecture confound; GSM1K correlation (Step 3) is relegated to 'suggestive but underpowered' in limitations.\"\n\n  - name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n    plan_id: plan_005\n    description: \"Bifactor IRT model fails to converge or produces inadmissible solutions on MMLU's 57 subjects, confirming IRT is fundamentally misspecified for LLM populations.\"\n    expected_results:\n      convergence: false\n      n_iterations_before_failure: 847\n      heywood_cases: 23\n      n_subjects_with_negative_variance: 8\n      reduced_model_5_factors_rmsea: 0.072\n      reduced_model_5_factors_cfi: 0.83\n      reduced_model_5_factors_srmr: 0.089\n      anchor_items_identified: 0\n      equating_feasible: false\n      fallback_unidimensional_rmsea: 0.061\n      fallback_unidimensional_cfi: 0.86\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, IRT is fundamentally misspecified for LLM populations. A 57-factor bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific) with 12,508 items and 5,219 'examinees' (models) is computationally massive and produces Heywood cases (negative variance estimates) because LLM response patterns violate local independence assumptions. Models within the same architecture family show correlated residuals that no factor structure can absorb. Even a reduced 5-factor exploratory model shows poor fit (RMSEA=0.072, CFI=0.83). Without a fitting bifactor model, anchor items cannot be identified (anchor selection requires items loading only on the general factor with known parameters), so IRT equating is impossible. The fallback unidimensional model is also misspecified (RMSEA=0.061, CFI=0.86). This experiment provides the strongest evidence that IRT-based approaches are inappropriate for LLM populations — not just DIF, but the entire psychometric framework.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Model fit comparison across specifications (unidimensional, 5-factor, 14-factor, full 57-factor bifactor). All show RMSEA>0.05, CFI<0.90.\"\n      - \"Table: Heywood cases by subject group. 23/57 subjects produce inadmissible parameter estimates.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Convergence trace: Log-likelihood vs iteration number. Shows oscillation after iteration 500, never stabilizing.\"\n      - \"Residual correlation heatmap: Items grouped by subject. Clear block-diagonal structure with substantial off-diagonal correlations that violate local independence.\"\n    analysis: \"This is a fundamental negative result. If IRT cannot be fit to LLM response data, then all IRT-based methods (MH-DIF, IRT equating, item banking) are built on sand. The failure mode is structural: LLM 'abilities' are not well-described by latent trait models because (1) within-architecture correlations violate independence, (2) multidimensional construct with 57 subjects exceeds feasible factor structures, (3) LLM response patterns (near-deterministic for easy items, architecture-dependent for hard items) violate the smooth ICC assumption. For the reframed paper, this is a KEY contribution: 'we empirically demonstrate that standard IRT is misspecified for LLM populations, with specific failure modes documented.' This motivates future work on LLM-specific psychometric models.\"\n\n  - name: item_generation_calibration\n    plan_id: plan_006\n    description: \"Item generation is moot because equating pilot (plan_005) failed — no anchor framework exists to calibrate replacement items against. Partial results from student simulation show moderate difficulty prediction but no equating guarantee.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_generated: 150\n      generation_quality_human_rating: 3.8/5\n      difficulty_correlation_prompt_based: 0.22\n      difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.61\n      difficulty_mae_student_sim: 0.18\n      equating_feasible: false\n      ranking_rho_original_vs_replaced: null\n      construct_coverage_maintained: \"unknown — no bifactor model to verify\"\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, this experiment is blocked by plan_005's failure. Without a bifactor model providing anchor items and factor loading targets, there's no principled way to calibrate replacement items. We can still generate items and estimate difficulty via student simulation (r=0.61, consistent with literature's r=0.75-0.82 but lower because we lack the anchoring framework). Prompt-based difficulty targeting performs poorly (r=0.22, consistent with arXiv 2602.06631's finding of 0.10-0.45). However, without equating, we cannot demonstrate score equivalence (the core goal). The generated items might be 'similarly difficult' but we cannot guarantee they measure the same construct — and given that IRT itself is misspecified, 'same construct' is undefined anyway. This experiment produces a modest methodological finding (student simulation > prompt-based difficulty targeting) but cannot achieve its primary objective.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Difficulty prediction accuracy by method (prompt-based vs student simulation vs empirical). Student simulation dominates but all methods have substantial error.\"\n      - \"Table: Generated item quality ratings by dimension (factual accuracy, appropriate difficulty, construct alignment, distractor quality).\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: Predicted vs actual difficulty for generated items. Student simulation shows moderate correlation (r=0.61) with high residual variance.\"\n      - \"Distribution comparison: Difficulty distribution of generated items vs DIF-flagged items they replace. Reasonable overlap but no formal equating guarantee.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment demonstrates that item generation is technically feasible but psychometrically ungrounded without a working equating framework. Under worst-case, this becomes a limitations discussion: 'IRT equating requires model fit; LLM populations violate IRT assumptions; therefore principled item replacement via classical equating is not currently feasible.' The student simulation finding (r=0.61) has standalone value as a practical heuristic for difficulty targeting, but falls short of the equating guarantee needed for Claim 3 (benchmark regeneration). Claim 3 is dropped entirely under worst-case.\"\n\n  - name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n    plan_id: plan_007\n    description: \"Bayesian accumulation of evidence from a misspecified model accumulates systematic bias rather than improving detection power. Cumulative posterior converges to wrong answer with increasing confidence.\"\n    expected_results:\n      individual_administration_power: 0.38\n      cumulative_power_after_2: 0.51\n      cumulative_power_after_3: 0.62\n      cumulative_power_after_4: 0.68\n      false_positive_rate_cumulative_4: 0.28\n      posterior_concentration_on_spurious_items: 0.73\n      prior_sensitivity_bayes_factor_range: [2.1, 8.4]\n      type_s_error_rate: 0.31\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, the Bayesian DIF model accumulates evidence for architecture-driven differential functioning, not contamination-driven. Because the underlying signal is real (architectures DO respond differently to items), Bayesian updating faithfully concentrates posterior probability on architecture-DIF items. Power 'improves' from 0.38 to 0.68, but this is power to detect the WRONG thing — architecture heterogeneity artifacts. The FPR (0.28 after 4 administrations) reflects the architecture confound persisting across all administrations (same architecture mix each time). Type-S error rate (0.31) shows that even when an item is correctly flagged as 'changed,' the direction (favoring which cohort) is often wrong because architecture effects are not monotonically temporal. This experiment demonstrates a subtle failure mode: Bayesian methods improve precision of estimation, but if the model is misspecified, they converge confidently to the wrong answer.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Power and FPR by number of accumulated administrations (1-4). Power increases but FPR remains stable at ~0.28 — accumulation doesn't resolve the architecture confound.\"\n      - \"Table: Type-S error analysis. 31% of flagged items show incorrect direction — Bayesian confidence without Bayesian accuracy.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Posterior evolution plot: 4 panels showing posterior DIF probability distributions for selected items across administrations. Posteriors concentrate but on architecture-artifact items.\"\n      - \"Calibration plot: Posterior probability vs empirical detection rate. Systematic overconfidence — model is well-calibrated if architecture effects ARE the target, but miscalibrated for contamination.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment reveals a deep problem: Bayesian sophistication cannot rescue a misspecified likelihood. If MH-DIF detects architecture effects rather than contamination, then accumulating MH evidence across time points produces increasingly confident architecture-DIF detection. The simulation shows 'improvement' in raw power but no improvement in the thing we actually care about (contamination detection). For the reframed paper, this result supports the narrative that 'more sophisticated statistical methods (Bayesian, IRT-based, cumulative) cannot fix a fundamental model misspecification problem — the issue is the DIF model itself, not the inference procedure.'\"\n\n  - name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n    plan_id: plan_008\n    description: \"Graceful degradation analysis is compromised because the items being 'replaced' were flagged by a misspecified method. Results show that removing random 31% of items has similar effect to removing DIF-flagged items — because DIF flags ARE approximately random w.r.t. item quality.\"\n    expected_results:\n      rho_5pct_replaced: 0.998\n      rho_10pct_replaced: 0.996\n      rho_20pct_replaced: 0.991\n      rho_50pct_replaced: 0.972\n      reliability_alpha_original: 0.94\n      reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced: 0.89\n      construct_coverage_50pct: 0.82\n      dif_removal_vs_random_removal_diff: 0.003\n      theoretical_bound_validity_loss_rate: 0.0015_per_percent\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, DIF flags are architecture noise — they don't identify 'bad' or 'contaminated' items, just items where different architectures respond differently. Removing DIF-flagged items (31%) produces ranking correlations (ρ=0.991 at 20%) nearly identical to removing 20% random items (ρ=0.989). The negligible difference (0.003) confirms that DIF flags have no special relationship to item quality or measurement validity — they're not the 'worst' items, just items sensitive to architecture. Construct coverage drops proportionally to removal rate, as expected for random removal. This result is actually CONSISTENT with plan_017's finding that DIF removal and random removal produce similar ranking stability — but reinterpreted: plan_017 showed DIF removal is MORE disruptive than random (z=-14.91), but under worst-case this is because architecture-sensitive items happen to be slightly more discriminating (they differentiate models), not because they're contaminated.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Validity metrics by replacement rate (5%, 10%, 20%, 50%). Graceful degradation regardless of which items are removed.\"\n      - \"Table: DIF-flagged removal vs random removal vs stratified-random removal (preserving subject proportions). Minimal differences across strategies.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Degradation curve: ρ vs replacement rate, with DIF-targeted and random-targeted lines overlapping almost perfectly.\"\n      - \"Reliability decline: Cronbach's α as function of items removed, showing expected ~0.001 per percent drop regardless of selection strategy.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment was designed to show 'principled replacement (DIF-targeted) maintains validity better than random replacement.' Under worst-case, it shows no such advantage — confirming that DIF flags don't identify validity-threatening items. For the reframed paper, this is reinterpreted as evidence of MMLU's massive redundancy (you can remove 31% of items with negligible ranking impact regardless of which items) rather than evidence that DIF-flagged items are specially problematic. Combined with plan_017's finding of ρ≈0.997, the message becomes: 'MMLU has enormous measurement redundancy — remove any 31% and rankings barely change.'\"\n\n  - name: item_focused_trees\n    plan_id: plan_009\n    description: \"Item-Focused Trees reveal that release date (continuous covariate) is LESS predictive than architecture type (categorical) — confirming architecture as the primary DIF driver.\"\n    expected_results:\n      ift_primary_split_variable: architecture_type\n      ift_release_date_importance_rank: 3\n      ift_architecture_importance_rank: 1\n      ift_parameter_count_importance_rank: 2\n      ift_n_items_with_date_split: 412\n      ift_n_items_with_arch_split: 3847\n      ift_vs_categorical_dif_agreement: 0.34\n      ift_c_pct_equivalent: 8.7\n      ift_interpretable_splits_pct: 62\n    result_rationale: \"Item-Focused Trees use recursive partitioning on model covariates (release date, architecture, parameter count, training method) to identify which covariates explain item response differences. Under worst-case, the trees predominantly split on architecture_type (3,847 items have architecture as primary split) rather than release_date (412 items). This directly demonstrates that temporal DIF is primarily an architecture confound — when given the option to split on time OR architecture, the tree algorithm overwhelmingly chooses architecture. The 'C% equivalent' using only temporal splits is 8.7% — dramatically lower than the 31% from forced temporal cohort DIF. Parameter count ranks second (proxy for architecture generation). Release date is third, often appearing only in deep splits after architecture is controlled. Agreement with categorical DIF (0.34) is low because IFT correctly attributes variance to architecture while categorical DIF conflates architecture with time.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Variable importance rankings across all items. Architecture dominates (mean Gini importance 0.43), parameter count second (0.28), release date third (0.17).\"\n      - \"Table: Items where release_date IS the primary split — clustered in subjects with genuine temporal curriculum effects (coding, recent events).\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Example IFT for a representative item: tree splits on architecture → parameter count → release_date, showing architecture absorbs most variance before time is considered.\"\n      - \"Variable importance distribution: Boxplot across 12,508 items showing architecture importance >> release_date importance.\"\n    analysis: \"This is perhaps the most DIAGNOSTIC experiment under worst-case. Item-Focused Trees provide a model-free, data-driven decomposition of what drives item response differences. The overwhelming dominance of architecture over release date as a splitting variable is direct evidence that temporal DIF is an architecture confound. The 8.7% of items where time IS the primary split (after architecture is controlled) represents the 'true' temporal instability — well within the worst-case range of 5-14%. For the reframed paper, IFT becomes a KEY methodological recommendation: 'use covariate-based DIF methods that can distinguish architecture effects from temporal effects, rather than forcing categorical temporal splits that conflate the two.'\"\n\n  - name: architecture_stratified_dif\n    plan_id: plan_010\n    description: \"THE critical experiment for worst-case. Decoder-only temporal DIF drops to 5-14%, IRT fit remains poor (RMSEA>0.05, CFI<0.90), confirming architecture heterogeneity was driving 31% and IRT is misspecified even within architecture.\"\n    expected_results:\n      decoder_only_n_models: 3412\n      decoder_only_n_ref_temporal: 891\n      decoder_only_n_foc_temporal: 487\n      decoder_only_temporal_c_pct: 9.7\n      decoder_only_irt_rmsea: 0.063\n      decoder_only_irt_cfi: 0.84\n      decoder_only_irt_tli: 0.82\n      decoder_only_irt_srmr: 0.071\n      full_population_irt_rmsea: 0.089\n      full_population_irt_cfi: 0.74\n      decoder_only_vs_full_c_pct_reduction: 21.6\n      decoder_only_sanity_c_pct: 0.15\n      decoder_only_enrichment: 0.98\n      partial_rho_controlling_domain_accuracy: 0.02\n      partial_rho_p_value: 0.67\n      non_uniform_dif_sibtest_c_pct: 14.2\n      non_uniform_mh_overlap_pct: 28\n      encoder_decoder_subset_c_pct: 41.3\n      moe_subset_c_pct: 35.8\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, restricting to decoder-only models removes the primary confound (architecture heterogeneity). C% drops from 31.3% to 9.7% — a 21.6pp reduction showing that 69% of the original DIF signal was architecture-driven. The remaining 9.7% is within the worst-case range [5, 14]. IRT fit improves (RMSEA: 0.089→0.063, CFI: 0.74→0.84) but remains poor — even within decoder-only, the unidimensional IRT model is misspecified because (a) MMLU spans 57 subjects (multidimensional construct), (b) decoder-only models still vary in parameter count, training data, and alignment method creating residual heterogeneity, (c) LLM response patterns violate monotone ICC assumptions. Partial correlation controlling domain accuracy drops to r=0.02, p=0.67 — ALL remaining DIF is explained by capability improvement across subjects, not item-level contamination. Non-uniform DIF (SIBTEST) finds 14.2% but only 28% overlaps with MH flags — substantial non-uniform DIF that MH misses, undermining MH as the primary detection method. The encoder-decoder and MoE subsets RETAIN high C% (41%, 36%) because they are small, architecture-mixed populations.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DIF statistics by architecture subgroup (decoder-only, encoder-decoder, MoE, full population). Clear pattern: homogeneous populations → lower C%.\"\n      - \"Table: IRT fit indices comparison (full population vs decoder-only vs encoder-decoder). Decoder-only improves but all remain in 'poor fit' range.\"\n      - \"Table: Non-uniform DIF (SIBTEST) vs uniform DIF (MH) cross-tabulation. 72% non-overlap demonstrates MH misses majority of non-uniform effects.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: C% by population composition. Full (31.3%) → decoder-only (9.7%) → decoder-only controlling accuracy (≈0%). Visual demonstrates architecture confound accounting for most signal.\"\n      - \"IRT fit comparison: RMSEA/CFI scatterplot for different subpopulations. All points remain in 'poor fit' quadrant but decoder-only moves toward acceptable.\"\n      - \"Partial correlation plot: DIF magnitude vs domain accuracy improvement, controlling for architecture. Flat relationship (r=0.02) — no residual instability after controlling both confounds.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment is the worst-case smoking gun. It demonstrates: (1) Architecture heterogeneity accounts for ~70% of temporal DIF — the 31% headline was an artifact of pooling heterogeneous populations. (2) Even within a homogeneous architecture, IRT remains misspecified (RMSEA=0.063>0.05) — the psychometric model is inappropriate for LLMs period, not just for mixed populations. (3) The residual 9.7% DIF within decoder-only is fully explained by domain-level accuracy improvement (partial ρ≈0) — there's no item-level differential functioning beyond 'models got better at some subjects.' (4) MH misses substantial non-uniform DIF (72% non-overlap with SIBTEST), meaning even the 9.7% is detected by a method blind to interaction effects. Collectively: 'DIF on LLM benchmarks primarily detects architecture differences, not temporal instability or contamination. After controlling for architecture and capability improvement, residual item-level DIF approaches zero.' This kills Claim 1 but provides the empirical foundation for the negative-result paper.\"\n\n  - name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: \"Semi-synthetic framework works as designed — injection creates detectable signal even in architecture-mixed population. This validates the FRAMEWORK (Claim 3) even though NATURAL DIF is artifactual (Claim 1 fails). ΔTPR is positive and dose-responsive.\"\n    expected_results:\n      standard_mh_tpr_p0: 0.31\n      standard_mh_tpr_p03: 0.52\n      standard_mh_tpr_p05: 0.81\n      standard_mh_tpr_p07: 0.97\n      standard_mh_tpr_p10: 1.0\n      standard_mh_fpr_baseline: 0.31\n      standard_mh_fpr_p10: 0.59\n      standard_mh_delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n      external_matching_tpr_p05: 0.83\n      external_matching_fpr_baseline: 0.31\n      external_matching_fpr_p10: 0.32\n      external_matching_delta_tpr_p10: 0.69\n      fpr_inflation_standard_matching: 0.28\n      fpr_inflation_external_matching: 0.01\n      dose_response_monotonic: true\n      n_injection_items: 150\n      n_clean_items: 350\n    result_rationale: \"The semi-synthetic framework creates REAL differential signal by construction — flipping focal group responses from incorrect→correct produces genuine DIF regardless of whether natural DIF is artifactual. Under worst-case, the framework validation is INDEPENDENT of whether Claim 1 holds. ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates that MH-DIF CAN detect differential contamination when it exists — the problem is that natural temporal DIF is dominated by architecture effects, not that MH is fundamentally broken. The dose-response (monotonically increasing TPR from p=0.3 to p=1.0) confirms expected behavior. FPR inflation with standard matching (+0.28) confirms matching variable contamination. External matching holds FPR stable (+0.01). This experiment's success is the foundation for reframing: 'the detection infrastructure works; the problem is population structure, not methodology.' Cross-subject matching (plan_016) extends this to within-subject settings with ΔFPR=0.000.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Detection metrics by injection probability (p=0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0) × matching strategy (standard, external). Dose-response pattern clear in both.\"\n      - \"Table: FPR by matching strategy across injection levels. Standard matching inflates; external matching stable.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dose-response curve: ΔTPR vs injection probability p. Monotonic increase from 0 to +0.69 (external matching). Confirms framework detects injected signal proportionally.\"\n      - \"FPR comparison: Standard vs external matching across injection levels. Divergence demonstrates matching variable contamination problem and its solution.\"\n    analysis: \"This is the experiment that SAVES the paper under worst-case. Even if natural temporal DIF is architecture noise (Claim 1 fails), the semi-synthetic framework demonstrates that: (1) DIF CAN detect differential contamination when properly engineered (ΔTPR>0.5), (2) the matching strategy matters enormously (external matching prevents FPR inflation), (3) the framework provides controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. For the reframed paper, this becomes the centerpiece of Claim 3: 'we built and validated a reusable infrastructure for evaluating any item-level contamination detector — demonstrated here with MH-DIF, but applicable to future methods that address the architecture confound we identified.' The framework is the lasting contribution; MH-DIF is shown to be the wrong specific instantiation.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17325,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17325},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c15210f9-8311-4c63-9cc5-dc75333c59ea","uuid":"e6a9a5f3-07dd-4a11-9d52-e3f0cf860180"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":288511,"duration_api_ms":289831,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: \"Claim 1 collapses as headline — architecture stratification reveals 31% temporal DIF was population heterogeneity artifact (decoder-only C% drops to 5-14% with RMSEA>0.05). Paper reframes as negative-result contribution: 'naive psychometric DIF transfer to LLM populations fails due to architecture heterogeneity and multidimensional construct violation; we empirically characterize why.' Surviving pillars: (1) the negative result itself documenting structural failure modes, (2) Claim 2 ground truth meta-critique (method-independent), (3) Claim 3 semi-synthetic framework as reusable infrastructure. Target ICLR 2027 as empirical analysis paper with intellectual honesty.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n    plan_id: plan_001\n    description: \"Full-population DIF on METABENCH MMLU — this experiment still yields ~31% C% because it uses the architecture-mixed population. The result is not 'wrong' but is later revealed by plan_010 to be an artifact of architecture heterogeneity.\"\n    expected_results:\n      temporal_ets_c_pct: 32.1\n      temporal_fpr_li_clean: 0.325\n      temporal_enrichment: 0.97\n      ability_ets_c_pct: 3.1\n      ability_enrichment: 1.15\n      within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 58.7\n      within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct: 41.2\n      domain_spearman_rho: 0.29\n      domain_spearman_p: 0.028\n      sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.21\n      temporal_ability_jaccard: 0.016\n      negative_disc_sensitivity_delta: 1.8\n    result_rationale: \"Full-population DIF operates on architecture-heterogeneous sample mixing encoder-decoder, decoder-only, and MoE models. Under worst-case, this heterogeneity is the PRIMARY driver of the 31% C% — different architectures respond to items differently not because of contamination or temporal drift, but because their information processing creates systematic DIF when pooled. The 31% is 'real' statistically but an artifact of population mixing. Enrichment≈1.0 because DIF flags are architecture-driven, orthogonal to web-overlap. Within-family DIF (Llama-2 vs Llama-3) remains high (~59%) because version jumps involve architecture changes (GQA, RoPE scaling) not just continued training. Sanity check remains near zero because random split preserves architecture proportions in both groups.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DIF detection by cohort definition × item filtering (4 conditions). Temporal cohort shows 30-33% C% regardless of negative-discrimination filtering — the signal is architecture-driven, not item-quality-driven.\"\n      - \"Table: Within-family DIF for qualifying pairs. Llama-2→3 shows 59% (major architecture revision), Mistral→Mixtral 41% (MoE transition). Both reflect architecture changes, not contamination.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: DIF effect size (Δ_MH) vs domain accuracy improvement. ρ=0.29 appears moderate but will be shown null after architecture control (plan_010).\"\n      - \"Heatmap: C% by MMLU subject × cohort definition. Temporal cohort shows uniformly high C% across all subjects — consistent with a global confound (architecture) rather than domain-specific contamination.\"\n    analysis: \"Results appear to support Claim 1 at face value — 31% temporal instability, non-trivial sanity check. However, the uniformity across domains (no strong clustering in 'known contaminated' subjects) and the within-family results tracking architecture changes rather than training data changes are early warning signs. The enrichment≈1.0 result is correctly interpreted as expected (proxy mismatch), but will later be reinterpreted: DIF flags don't correlate with ANYTHING meaningful because they're architecture noise. This experiment provides the 'naive application' baseline that plan_010 deconstructs.\"\n\n  - name: rl_robustness_validation\n    plan_id: plan_002\n    description: \"RL robustness validation becomes uninformative because the underlying DIF method is misspecified. Group-level DIF on architecture-mixed cohorts with or without RL models produces similarly inflated C% — testing 'robustness' of a broken method.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sft_only_cohort_c_pct: 29.4\n      alignment_rl_cohort_c_pct: 30.8\n      reasoning_rl_added_cohort_c_pct: 31.5\n      dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_rl: 0.72\n      auroc_dif_vs_constat: 0.53\n      auroc_dif_vs_mink: 0.51\n      auroc_ci_width: 0.18\n      delong_p_dif_vs_constat: 0.61\n      delong_p_dif_vs_mink: 0.74\n      tost_equivalence_p: 0.08\n      power_analysis_detection_boundary_p: 0.55\n      n_reasoning_rl_models_available: 13\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, DIF produces ~30% C% regardless of cohort composition because the signal is architecture-driven, not RL-driven. Adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a focal cohort of 656 models changes composition negligibly — C% moves by <2pp. The 'stability' across cohort compositions (sft/alignment-rl/reasoning-rl) is not evidence of robustness but of the method's insensitivity to the actual manipulation. AUROC comparisons against ConStat/Min-K% are near chance (0.51-0.53) because DIF flags are effectively random w.r.t. actual contamination. TOST equivalence test marginally fails (p=0.08) because the comparison is underpowered with N=13 RL models, not because there's a real difference. The entire experiment is measuring properties of a misspecified method — 'robust' misspecification is not a virtue.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: C% by cohort composition (SFT-only, +Alignment-RL, +Reasoning-RL). Shows <2pp variation — interpretable as robustness OR as insensitivity to meaningful signal.\"\n      - \"Table: Per-model detection comparison (DIF flag membership vs ConStat/Min-K% scores) for 13 reasoning-RL models. Near-chance discrimination.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curves: DIF vs ConStat vs Min-K% on the 13 reasoning-RL models. All curves near diagonal — none of the methods reliably detect contamination at the individual model level with N=13.\"\n      - \"Power curve: Detection probability vs exploitation rate p. Shows DIF 'detection boundary' at p≈0.55, but this is against injected contamination (plan_011 style), not real RL-concealed contamination.\"\n    analysis: \"The experiment is interpretable two ways: (1) DIF is robust to RL (flags same items regardless of RL presence), or (2) DIF flags are architecture noise unaffected by any real signal. Under worst-case, interpretation (2) dominates. The N=13 reasoning-RL sample is too small for definitive conclusions either way, which is acknowledged. The AUROC comparisons show DIF has no advantage over other methods for per-model detection — but this was never DIF's design (group-level detector). The entire premise of 'RL robustness' becomes moot when the method doesn't detect real contamination in the first place. This experiment converts from a strength argument to an additional data point in the negative-result narrative.\"\n\n  - name: constat_perturbation_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003a\n    description: \"ConStat perturbation testing provides moderate standalone detection on non-RL models, but DIF comparison is meaningless because DIF's flags are architecture artifacts.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_reformulated: 217\n      constat_auroc_non_rl: 0.68\n      constat_auroc_rl: 0.44\n      constat_fdr_controlled_detections: 34\n      constat_detection_rate: 0.157\n      dif_constat_overlap_pct: 18\n      dif_constat_jaccard: 0.09\n      reformulation_quality_human_rating: 4.1/5\n      effect_size_distribution_median: 0.42\n      effect_size_distribution_iqr: [0.18, 0.71]\n    result_rationale: \"ConStat operates on a fundamentally different mechanism (within-model perturbation sensitivity) than DIF (between-group differential). Under worst-case, ConStat shows moderate AUROC (0.68) on non-RL models because perturbation sensitivity is a real contamination signal — memorized answers are brittle to reformulation. The RL degradation (0.44) confirms Wang et al.'s theoretical prediction. The critical finding is the near-zero overlap between ConStat detections and DIF flags (Jaccard=0.09). Under the 'best' interpretation, this means they detect complementary signals. Under worst-case, it means DIF flags are random w.r.t. actual contamination (confirmed by enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al.). ConStat provides standalone value but the DIF-ConStat complementarity narrative collapses — you can't build an ensemble when one component is noise.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: ConStat detection rates by effect size bin (small/medium/large). Large effects detected at 65%, medium at 28%, small at 4%.\"\n      - \"Table: Cross-tabulation of DIF flags × ConStat detections. Near-independence (χ²=1.3, p=0.25) — consistent with DIF being architecture noise.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curve: ConStat performance stratified by model type (base, SFT, alignment-RL, reasoning-RL). Clear degradation curve from base (0.74) to reasoning-RL (0.44).\"\n      - \"Venn diagram: DIF-C items vs ConStat detections. Minimal overlap, visually reinforcing independence.\"\n    analysis: \"ConStat provides a legitimate contamination signal (perturbation sensitivity is mechanistically grounded) but lives in a different detection paradigm than DIF. Under worst-case, this experiment inadvertently strengthens the case that DIF flags are not contamination-related — an independent contamination detector shows near-zero agreement with DIF. For the reframed paper, ConStat results join the 'what works' section while DIF joins the 'what fails and why' section. ConStat's RL vulnerability (AUROC 0.44) remains a real limitation that the paper can discuss.\"\n\n  - name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n    plan_id: plan_003b\n    description: \"DVD generation variance shows bimodal contamination signal for verbatim memorization but cannot be meaningfully compared to DIF because DIF is misspecified.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_models_evaluated: 12\n      n_items_evaluated: 500\n      dvd_auroc_verbatim: 0.61\n      dvd_auroc_variant: 0.47\n      bimodal_detection_rate: 0.083\n      generation_variance_clean_mean: 0.34\n      generation_variance_contaminated_mean: 0.21\n      dif_dvd_overlap_pct: 14\n      dif_dvd_jaccard: 0.06\n      temperature_sensitivity: \"T=0.7 AUROC=0.58, T=1.0 AUROC=0.61\"\n      compute_cost_per_model_hours: 6.2\n    result_rationale: \"DVD detects verbatim memorization (low generation variance = deterministic recall of memorized answer) at moderate AUROC (0.61). Variant contamination (paraphrased training exposure) is undetectable (0.47, below chance noise). The bimodal detection rate (8.3%) is low because most models don't exhibit extreme verbatim memorization on MMLU — MMLU items are widely available, so 'contamination' is ubiquitous and doesn't produce sharp variance signatures. DIF-DVD overlap (Jaccard=0.06) is near zero, again consistent with DIF flags being architecture noise rather than contamination signal. The compute cost (6.2 GPU-hours per model × 12 models ≈ 74 hours) is within budget but limits scalability. Under worst-case, DVD provides mild standalone signal but is insufficient for the ensemble premise.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DVD detection metrics by contamination type (verbatim vs variant vs clean). Only verbatim shows separation.\"\n      - \"Table: Generation variance distributions per model (12 models × summary stats). Models with known training data overlap show slightly lower variance but effect is small.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Histogram: Generation variance scores for known-contaminated vs clean items. Mild leftward shift for contaminated items, heavy overlap.\"\n      - \"Bimodal detection example: one model showing clear bimodal variance distribution on ~20 items (cherry-picked best case).\"\n    analysis: \"DVD's theoretical mechanism (memorized items produce low generation variance) is sound but MMLU's universal exposure means the contrast between 'contaminated' and 'clean' items is weak. Under worst-case, DVD provides marginal standalone signal (AUROC 0.61) — better than DIF's effective chance performance but insufficient to carry a detection claim. The near-zero DIF overlap is another nail in DIF's coffin. For the reframed paper, DVD becomes a 'methods we evaluated' entry rather than an ensemble component.\"\n\n  - name: ensemble_fusion\n    plan_id: plan_003c\n    description: \"Ensemble fusion fails because the DIF component is noise and the remaining two signals (ConStat + DVD) are individually weak, producing an ensemble barely above the better single method.\"\n    expected_results:\n      ensemble_auroc_2of3: 0.52\n      ensemble_auroc_weighted: 0.63\n      ensemble_auroc_constat_dvd_only: 0.66\n      ensemble_fpr_2of3: 0.04\n      ensemble_fpr_weighted: 0.11\n      dif_weight_optimized: 0.03\n      constat_weight_optimized: 0.62\n      dvd_weight_optimized: 0.35\n      cross_val_auroc_std: 0.09\n      uniform_weight_auroc: 0.55\n      ensemble_vs_constat_alone_improvement: 0.02\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, the ensemble is dominated by ConStat because DIF's optimized weight drops to ~0.03 (effectively excluded by cross-validation). 2-of-3 majority voting produces AUROC=0.52 because DIF is a random voter — it agrees with the correct answer by chance 50% of the time, degrading consensus. Weighted fusion (0.63) barely exceeds ConStat alone (0.68 standalone vs 0.66 in the ConStat+DVD pair) because DVD adds noise rather than complementary signal for non-verbatim contamination. The ensemble doesn't exceed the best single component — a classic sign that the 'complementary coverage' hypothesis is wrong. Cross-validation variance (std=0.09) is high because the small calibration set (few items with known contamination) produces unstable weights. This experiment's failure is a direct consequence of DIF's misspecification propagating through the ensemble.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Ensemble performance by configuration (2-of-3, weighted, ConStat+DVD only, each alone). Shows no ensemble benefit over best single method.\"\n      - \"Table: Cross-validated weights across 5 folds. DIF weight ranges 0.00-0.08 across folds — consistently suppressed.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"ROC curves: Individual methods vs ensemble configurations. Ensemble curves cluster around ConStat's curve, never exceeding it significantly.\"\n      - \"Calibration plot: Predicted contamination probability vs observed rate. Poor calibration due to small calibration set.\"\n    analysis: \"The ensemble premise required three complementary detectors covering different contamination regimes. Under worst-case, this collapses: DIF is architecture noise (not a contamination detector at all), DVD is marginal (AUROC 0.61), and only ConStat provides real signal (AUROC 0.68 on non-RL). The ensemble reduces to 'ConStat with extra steps.' For the reframed paper, this negative result strengthens the 'DIF doesn't work for this purpose' narrative — even optimistic fusion cannot rescue a misspecified component. The experiment is dropped from claims but reported in supplementary as additional evidence for the negative result.\"\n\n  - name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n    plan_id: plan_004\n    description: \"GSM8K replication still shows semi-synthetic framework works (infrastructure contribution survives) but temporal DIF on GSM8K is reinterpreted as reflecting GSM8K's simpler architecture landscape.\"\n    expected_results:\n      step1_gsm8k_temporal_c_pct: 12.3\n      step1_gsm8k_external_c_pct: 38.7\n      step1_gsm8k_sanity_c_pct: 0.4\n      step2_standard_delta_tpr_p10: 0.78\n      step2_external_delta_tpr_p10: 0.49\n      step3_n_models: 33\n      step3_pearson_r: -0.31\n      step3_pearson_p: 0.08\n      step3_spearman_rho: -0.28\n      step3_spearman_p: 0.11\n      step3_partial_r_controlling_accuracy: -0.22\n      step3_partial_p: 0.21\n      step3_permutation_p: 0.09\n      step3_ci_95_lower: -0.58\n      step3_ci_95_upper: 0.03\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, GSM8K temporal DIF drops to 12.3% (vs MMLU's 31%) because GSM8K's model population is more architecturally homogeneous (primarily decoder-only math models). This actually supports the architecture-artifact hypothesis — less architecture mixing → less spurious DIF. The semi-synthetic framework (Step 2) still shows ΔTPR=+0.78 because injection creates real differential signal detectable by MH regardless of architecture mixing — this confirms the framework works even if natural DIF is artifactual. Step 3 (GSM1K correlation) weakens substantially: r=-0.31 with p=0.08 (marginally non-significant), CI crossing zero [-0.58, 0.03]. The N=33 sample that was borderline in the 'best' scenario becomes non-significant under worst-case measurement noise. Partial correlation controlling accuracy drops to r=-0.22, p=0.21 — clearly non-significant. The GSM1K finding cannot carry any weight in the paper.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: GSM8K DIF summary statistics vs MMLU DIF. Lower C% on more architecturally homogeneous population supports architecture confound explanation.\"\n      - \"Table: GSM1K correlation analysis with confidence intervals. CI crosses zero — finding is suggestive at best.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: Per-model contamination gap vs DIF-C item performance. Trend line with wide CI band crossing zero. Individual points show high scatter.\"\n      - \"Dose-response curve: Semi-synthetic ΔTPR vs injection probability p. Clear monotonic increase — framework validation survives regardless of natural DIF interpretation.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment provides mixed evidence under worst-case. The semi-synthetic framework (Claim 3) is fully validated — injection creates detectable signal, confirming the infrastructure works. But temporal DIF on GSM8K (12.3%) aligns with the architecture-artifact story (less architecture mixing → less spurious DIF). GSM1K correlation becomes non-significant (p=0.08, CI crosses zero), removing the cross-benchmark contamination validation. For the reframed paper: framework portability (Step 2) is a positive result supporting Claim 3; temporal DIF comparison (Step 1) becomes additional evidence for the architecture confound; GSM1K correlation (Step 3) is relegated to 'suggestive but underpowered' in limitations.\"\n\n  - name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n    plan_id: plan_005\n    description: \"Bifactor IRT model fails to converge or produces inadmissible solutions on MMLU's 57 subjects, confirming IRT is fundamentally misspecified for LLM populations.\"\n    expected_results:\n      convergence: false\n      n_iterations_before_failure: 847\n      heywood_cases: 23\n      n_subjects_with_negative_variance: 8\n      reduced_model_5_factors_rmsea: 0.072\n      reduced_model_5_factors_cfi: 0.83\n      reduced_model_5_factors_srmr: 0.089\n      anchor_items_identified: 0\n      equating_feasible: false\n      fallback_unidimensional_rmsea: 0.061\n      fallback_unidimensional_cfi: 0.86\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, IRT is fundamentally misspecified for LLM populations. A 57-factor bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific) with 12,508 items and 5,219 'examinees' (models) is computationally massive and produces Heywood cases (negative variance estimates) because LLM response patterns violate local independence assumptions. Models within the same architecture family show correlated residuals that no factor structure can absorb. Even a reduced 5-factor exploratory model shows poor fit (RMSEA=0.072, CFI=0.83). Without a fitting bifactor model, anchor items cannot be identified (anchor selection requires items loading only on the general factor with known parameters), so IRT equating is impossible. The fallback unidimensional model is also misspecified (RMSEA=0.061, CFI=0.86). This experiment provides the strongest evidence that IRT-based approaches are inappropriate for LLM populations — not just DIF, but the entire psychometric framework.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Model fit comparison across specifications (unidimensional, 5-factor, 14-factor, full 57-factor bifactor). All show RMSEA>0.05, CFI<0.90.\"\n      - \"Table: Heywood cases by subject group. 23/57 subjects produce inadmissible parameter estimates.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Convergence trace: Log-likelihood vs iteration number. Shows oscillation after iteration 500, never stabilizing.\"\n      - \"Residual correlation heatmap: Items grouped by subject. Clear block-diagonal structure with substantial off-diagonal correlations that violate local independence.\"\n    analysis: \"This is a fundamental negative result. If IRT cannot be fit to LLM response data, then all IRT-based methods (MH-DIF, IRT equating, item banking) are built on sand. The failure mode is structural: LLM 'abilities' are not well-described by latent trait models because (1) within-architecture correlations violate independence, (2) multidimensional construct with 57 subjects exceeds feasible factor structures, (3) LLM response patterns (near-deterministic for easy items, architecture-dependent for hard items) violate the smooth ICC assumption. For the reframed paper, this is a KEY contribution: 'we empirically demonstrate that standard IRT is misspecified for LLM populations, with specific failure modes documented.' This motivates future work on LLM-specific psychometric models.\"\n\n  - name: item_generation_calibration\n    plan_id: plan_006\n    description: \"Item generation is moot because equating pilot (plan_005) failed — no anchor framework exists to calibrate replacement items against. Partial results from student simulation show moderate difficulty prediction but no equating guarantee.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_items_generated: 150\n      generation_quality_human_rating: 3.8/5\n      difficulty_correlation_prompt_based: 0.22\n      difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.61\n      difficulty_mae_student_sim: 0.18\n      equating_feasible: false\n      ranking_rho_original_vs_replaced: null\n      construct_coverage_maintained: \"unknown — no bifactor model to verify\"\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, this experiment is blocked by plan_005's failure. Without a bifactor model providing anchor items and factor loading targets, there's no principled way to calibrate replacement items. We can still generate items and estimate difficulty via student simulation (r=0.61, consistent with literature's r=0.75-0.82 but lower because we lack the anchoring framework). Prompt-based difficulty targeting performs poorly (r=0.22, consistent with arXiv 2602.06631's finding of 0.10-0.45). However, without equating, we cannot demonstrate score equivalence (the core goal). The generated items might be 'similarly difficult' but we cannot guarantee they measure the same construct — and given that IRT itself is misspecified, 'same construct' is undefined anyway. This experiment produces a modest methodological finding (student simulation > prompt-based difficulty targeting) but cannot achieve its primary objective.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Difficulty prediction accuracy by method (prompt-based vs student simulation vs empirical). Student simulation dominates but all methods have substantial error.\"\n      - \"Table: Generated item quality ratings by dimension (factual accuracy, appropriate difficulty, construct alignment, distractor quality).\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Scatter plot: Predicted vs actual difficulty for generated items. Student simulation shows moderate correlation (r=0.61) with high residual variance.\"\n      - \"Distribution comparison: Difficulty distribution of generated items vs DIF-flagged items they replace. Reasonable overlap but no formal equating guarantee.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment demonstrates that item generation is technically feasible but psychometrically ungrounded without a working equating framework. Under worst-case, this becomes a limitations discussion: 'IRT equating requires model fit; LLM populations violate IRT assumptions; therefore principled item replacement via classical equating is not currently feasible.' The student simulation finding (r=0.61) has standalone value as a practical heuristic for difficulty targeting, but falls short of the equating guarantee needed for Claim 3 (benchmark regeneration). Claim 3 is dropped entirely under worst-case.\"\n\n  - name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n    plan_id: plan_007\n    description: \"Bayesian accumulation of evidence from a misspecified model accumulates systematic bias rather than improving detection power. Cumulative posterior converges to wrong answer with increasing confidence.\"\n    expected_results:\n      individual_administration_power: 0.38\n      cumulative_power_after_2: 0.51\n      cumulative_power_after_3: 0.62\n      cumulative_power_after_4: 0.68\n      false_positive_rate_cumulative_4: 0.28\n      posterior_concentration_on_spurious_items: 0.73\n      prior_sensitivity_bayes_factor_range: [2.1, 8.4]\n      type_s_error_rate: 0.31\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, the Bayesian DIF model accumulates evidence for architecture-driven differential functioning, not contamination-driven. Because the underlying signal is real (architectures DO respond differently to items), Bayesian updating faithfully concentrates posterior probability on architecture-DIF items. Power 'improves' from 0.38 to 0.68, but this is power to detect the WRONG thing — architecture heterogeneity artifacts. The FPR (0.28 after 4 administrations) reflects the architecture confound persisting across all administrations (same architecture mix each time). Type-S error rate (0.31) shows that even when an item is correctly flagged as 'changed,' the direction (favoring which cohort) is often wrong because architecture effects are not monotonically temporal. This experiment demonstrates a subtle failure mode: Bayesian methods improve precision of estimation, but if the model is misspecified, they converge confidently to the wrong answer.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Power and FPR by number of accumulated administrations (1-4). Power increases but FPR remains stable at ~0.28 — accumulation doesn't resolve the architecture confound.\"\n      - \"Table: Type-S error analysis. 31% of flagged items show incorrect direction — Bayesian confidence without Bayesian accuracy.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Posterior evolution plot: 4 panels showing posterior DIF probability distributions for selected items across administrations. Posteriors concentrate but on architecture-artifact items.\"\n      - \"Calibration plot: Posterior probability vs empirical detection rate. Systematic overconfidence — model is well-calibrated if architecture effects ARE the target, but miscalibrated for contamination.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment reveals a deep problem: Bayesian sophistication cannot rescue a misspecified likelihood. If MH-DIF detects architecture effects rather than contamination, then accumulating MH evidence across time points produces increasingly confident architecture-DIF detection. The simulation shows 'improvement' in raw power but no improvement in the thing we actually care about (contamination detection). For the reframed paper, this result supports the narrative that 'more sophisticated statistical methods (Bayesian, IRT-based, cumulative) cannot fix a fundamental model misspecification problem — the issue is the DIF model itself, not the inference procedure.'\"\n\n  - name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n    plan_id: plan_008\n    description: \"Graceful degradation analysis is compromised because the items being 'replaced' were flagged by a misspecified method. Results show that removing random 31% of items has similar effect to removing DIF-flagged items — because DIF flags ARE approximately random w.r.t. item quality.\"\n    expected_results:\n      rho_5pct_replaced: 0.998\n      rho_10pct_replaced: 0.996\n      rho_20pct_replaced: 0.991\n      rho_50pct_replaced: 0.972\n      reliability_alpha_original: 0.94\n      reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced: 0.89\n      construct_coverage_50pct: 0.82\n      dif_removal_vs_random_removal_diff: 0.003\n      theoretical_bound_validity_loss_rate: 0.0015_per_percent\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, DIF flags are architecture noise — they don't identify 'bad' or 'contaminated' items, just items where different architectures respond differently. Removing DIF-flagged items (31%) produces ranking correlations (ρ=0.991 at 20%) nearly identical to removing 20% random items (ρ=0.989). The negligible difference (0.003) confirms that DIF flags have no special relationship to item quality or measurement validity — they're not the 'worst' items, just items sensitive to architecture. Construct coverage drops proportionally to removal rate, as expected for random removal. This result is actually CONSISTENT with plan_017's finding that DIF removal and random removal produce similar ranking stability — but reinterpreted: plan_017 showed DIF removal is MORE disruptive than random (z=-14.91), but under worst-case this is because architecture-sensitive items happen to be slightly more discriminating (they differentiate models), not because they're contaminated.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Validity metrics by replacement rate (5%, 10%, 20%, 50%). Graceful degradation regardless of which items are removed.\"\n      - \"Table: DIF-flagged removal vs random removal vs stratified-random removal (preserving subject proportions). Minimal differences across strategies.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Degradation curve: ρ vs replacement rate, with DIF-targeted and random-targeted lines overlapping almost perfectly.\"\n      - \"Reliability decline: Cronbach's α as function of items removed, showing expected ~0.001 per percent drop regardless of selection strategy.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment was designed to show 'principled replacement (DIF-targeted) maintains validity better than random replacement.' Under worst-case, it shows no such advantage — confirming that DIF flags don't identify validity-threatening items. For the reframed paper, this is reinterpreted as evidence of MMLU's massive redundancy (you can remove 31% of items with negligible ranking impact regardless of which items) rather than evidence that DIF-flagged items are specially problematic. Combined with plan_017's finding of ρ≈0.997, the message becomes: 'MMLU has enormous measurement redundancy — remove any 31% and rankings barely change.'\"\n\n  - name: item_focused_trees\n    plan_id: plan_009\n    description: \"Item-Focused Trees reveal that release date (continuous covariate) is LESS predictive than architecture type (categorical) — confirming architecture as the primary DIF driver.\"\n    expected_results:\n      ift_primary_split_variable: architecture_type\n      ift_release_date_importance_rank: 3\n      ift_architecture_importance_rank: 1\n      ift_parameter_count_importance_rank: 2\n      ift_n_items_with_date_split: 412\n      ift_n_items_with_arch_split: 3847\n      ift_vs_categorical_dif_agreement: 0.34\n      ift_c_pct_equivalent: 8.7\n      ift_interpretable_splits_pct: 62\n    result_rationale: \"Item-Focused Trees use recursive partitioning on model covariates (release date, architecture, parameter count, training method) to identify which covariates explain item response differences. Under worst-case, the trees predominantly split on architecture_type (3,847 items have architecture as primary split) rather than release_date (412 items). This directly demonstrates that temporal DIF is primarily an architecture confound — when given the option to split on time OR architecture, the tree algorithm overwhelmingly chooses architecture. The 'C% equivalent' using only temporal splits is 8.7% — dramatically lower than the 31% from forced temporal cohort DIF. Parameter count ranks second (proxy for architecture generation). Release date is third, often appearing only in deep splits after architecture is controlled. Agreement with categorical DIF (0.34) is low because IFT correctly attributes variance to architecture while categorical DIF conflates architecture with time.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Variable importance rankings across all items. Architecture dominates (mean Gini importance 0.43), parameter count second (0.28), release date third (0.17).\"\n      - \"Table: Items where release_date IS the primary split — clustered in subjects with genuine temporal curriculum effects (coding, recent events).\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Example IFT for a representative item: tree splits on architecture → parameter count → release_date, showing architecture absorbs most variance before time is considered.\"\n      - \"Variable importance distribution: Boxplot across 12,508 items showing architecture importance >> release_date importance.\"\n    analysis: \"This is perhaps the most DIAGNOSTIC experiment under worst-case. Item-Focused Trees provide a model-free, data-driven decomposition of what drives item response differences. The overwhelming dominance of architecture over release date as a splitting variable is direct evidence that temporal DIF is an architecture confound. The 8.7% of items where time IS the primary split (after architecture is controlled) represents the 'true' temporal instability — well within the worst-case range of 5-14%. For the reframed paper, IFT becomes a KEY methodological recommendation: 'use covariate-based DIF methods that can distinguish architecture effects from temporal effects, rather than forcing categorical temporal splits that conflate the two.'\"\n\n  - name: architecture_stratified_dif\n    plan_id: plan_010\n    description: \"THE critical experiment for worst-case. Decoder-only temporal DIF drops to 5-14%, IRT fit remains poor (RMSEA>0.05, CFI<0.90), confirming architecture heterogeneity was driving 31% and IRT is misspecified even within architecture.\"\n    expected_results:\n      decoder_only_n_models: 3412\n      decoder_only_n_ref_temporal: 891\n      decoder_only_n_foc_temporal: 487\n      decoder_only_temporal_c_pct: 9.7\n      decoder_only_irt_rmsea: 0.063\n      decoder_only_irt_cfi: 0.84\n      decoder_only_irt_tli: 0.82\n      decoder_only_irt_srmr: 0.071\n      full_population_irt_rmsea: 0.089\n      full_population_irt_cfi: 0.74\n      decoder_only_vs_full_c_pct_reduction: 21.6\n      decoder_only_sanity_c_pct: 0.15\n      decoder_only_enrichment: 0.98\n      partial_rho_controlling_domain_accuracy: 0.02\n      partial_rho_p_value: 0.67\n      non_uniform_dif_sibtest_c_pct: 14.2\n      non_uniform_mh_overlap_pct: 28\n      encoder_decoder_subset_c_pct: 41.3\n      moe_subset_c_pct: 35.8\n    result_rationale: \"Under worst-case, restricting to decoder-only models removes the primary confound (architecture heterogeneity). C% drops from 31.3% to 9.7% — a 21.6pp reduction showing that 69% of the original DIF signal was architecture-driven. The remaining 9.7% is within the worst-case range [5, 14]. IRT fit improves (RMSEA: 0.089→0.063, CFI: 0.74→0.84) but remains poor — even within decoder-only, the unidimensional IRT model is misspecified because (a) MMLU spans 57 subjects (multidimensional construct), (b) decoder-only models still vary in parameter count, training data, and alignment method creating residual heterogeneity, (c) LLM response patterns violate monotone ICC assumptions. Partial correlation controlling domain accuracy drops to r=0.02, p=0.67 — ALL remaining DIF is explained by capability improvement across subjects, not item-level contamination. Non-uniform DIF (SIBTEST) finds 14.2% but only 28% overlaps with MH flags — substantial non-uniform DIF that MH misses, undermining MH as the primary detection method. The encoder-decoder and MoE subsets RETAIN high C% (41%, 36%) because they are small, architecture-mixed populations.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: DIF statistics by architecture subgroup (decoder-only, encoder-decoder, MoE, full population). Clear pattern: homogeneous populations → lower C%.\"\n      - \"Table: IRT fit indices comparison (full population vs decoder-only vs encoder-decoder). Decoder-only improves but all remain in 'poor fit' range.\"\n      - \"Table: Non-uniform DIF (SIBTEST) vs uniform DIF (MH) cross-tabulation. 72% non-overlap demonstrates MH misses majority of non-uniform effects.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: C% by population composition. Full (31.3%) → decoder-only (9.7%) → decoder-only controlling accuracy (≈0%). Visual demonstrates architecture confound accounting for most signal.\"\n      - \"IRT fit comparison: RMSEA/CFI scatterplot for different subpopulations. All points remain in 'poor fit' quadrant but decoder-only moves toward acceptable.\"\n      - \"Partial correlation plot: DIF magnitude vs domain accuracy improvement, controlling for architecture. Flat relationship (r=0.02) — no residual instability after controlling both confounds.\"\n    analysis: \"This experiment is the worst-case smoking gun. It demonstrates: (1) Architecture heterogeneity accounts for ~70% of temporal DIF — the 31% headline was an artifact of pooling heterogeneous populations. (2) Even within a homogeneous architecture, IRT remains misspecified (RMSEA=0.063>0.05) — the psychometric model is inappropriate for LLMs period, not just for mixed populations. (3) The residual 9.7% DIF within decoder-only is fully explained by domain-level accuracy improvement (partial ρ≈0) — there's no item-level differential functioning beyond 'models got better at some subjects.' (4) MH misses substantial non-uniform DIF (72% non-overlap with SIBTEST), meaning even the 9.7% is detected by a method blind to interaction effects. Collectively: 'DIF on LLM benchmarks primarily detects architecture differences, not temporal instability or contamination. After controlling for architecture and capability improvement, residual item-level DIF approaches zero.' This kills Claim 1 but provides the empirical foundation for the negative-result paper.\"\n\n  - name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n    plan_id: plan_011\n    description: \"Semi-synthetic framework works as designed — injection creates detectable signal even in architecture-mixed population. This validates the FRAMEWORK (Claim 3) even though NATURAL DIF is artifactual (Claim 1 fails). ΔTPR is positive and dose-responsive.\"\n    expected_results:\n      standard_mh_tpr_p0: 0.31\n      standard_mh_tpr_p03: 0.52\n      standard_mh_tpr_p05: 0.81\n      standard_mh_tpr_p07: 0.97\n      standard_mh_tpr_p10: 1.0\n      standard_mh_fpr_baseline: 0.31\n      standard_mh_fpr_p10: 0.59\n      standard_mh_delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n      external_matching_tpr_p05: 0.83\n      external_matching_fpr_baseline: 0.31\n      external_matching_fpr_p10: 0.32\n      external_matching_delta_tpr_p10: 0.69\n      fpr_inflation_standard_matching: 0.28\n      fpr_inflation_external_matching: 0.01\n      dose_response_monotonic: true\n      n_injection_items: 150\n      n_clean_items: 350\n    result_rationale: \"The semi-synthetic framework creates REAL differential signal by construction — flipping focal group responses from incorrect→correct produces genuine DIF regardless of whether natural DIF is artifactual. Under worst-case, the framework validation is INDEPENDENT of whether Claim 1 holds. ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates that MH-DIF CAN detect differential contamination when it exists — the problem is that natural temporal DIF is dominated by architecture effects, not that MH is fundamentally broken. The dose-response (monotonically increasing TPR from p=0.3 to p=1.0) confirms expected behavior. FPR inflation with standard matching (+0.28) confirms matching variable contamination. External matching holds FPR stable (+0.01). This experiment's success is the foundation for reframing: 'the detection infrastructure works; the problem is population structure, not methodology.' Cross-subject matching (plan_016) extends this to within-subject settings with ΔFPR=0.000.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Table: Detection metrics by injection probability (p=0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0) × matching strategy (standard, external). Dose-response pattern clear in both.\"\n      - \"Table: FPR by matching strategy across injection levels. Standard matching inflates; external matching stable.\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dose-response curve: ΔTPR vs injection probability p. Monotonic increase from 0 to +0.69 (external matching). Confirms framework detects injected signal proportionally.\"\n      - \"FPR comparison: Standard vs external matching across injection levels. Divergence demonstrates matching variable contamination problem and its solution.\"\n    analysis: \"This is the experiment that SAVES the paper under worst-case. Even if natural temporal DIF is architecture noise (Claim 1 fails), the semi-synthetic framework demonstrates that: (1) DIF CAN detect differential contamination when properly engineered (ΔTPR>0.5), (2) the matching strategy matters enormously (external matching prevents FPR inflation), (3) the framework provides controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. For the reframed paper, this becomes the centerpiece of Claim 3: 'we built and validated a reusable infrastructure for evaluating any item-level contamination detector — demonstrated here with MH-DIF, but applicable to future methods that address the architecture confound we identified.' The framework is the lasting contribution; MH-DIF is shown to be the wrong specific instantiation.\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"c15210f9-8311-4c63-9cc5-dc75333c59ea","total_cost_usd":0.44202775,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17325,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":12617,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17325,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12617,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17325},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":12291,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.012391,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":12617,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":17325,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.42963675,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"b9c0ccb8-3a62-4f07-ad0d-5f2b1949d82d"}
